# Shell scripts must keep LF line endings no matter what core.autocrlf says
# on a given checkout — a CRLF shebang ("#!/usr/bin/env bash\r") fails on
# macOS/Linux with "bad interpreter: no such file or directory". These are
# double-clicked directly off a Windows-authored checkout (via the shared
# drive) rather than always going through a fresh git clone, so this can't
# rely on a one-time correct state — it has to hold permanently.
*.command text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# And the reverse for Windows batch files: cmd.exe reads .bat files in
# chunks and mis-parses LF-only lines, especially next to multi-byte UTF-8
# characters. tools/build/build_windows.bat did exactly that (2026-10-03),
# failing with fragments like "'ho' is not recognized as a command". Keep
# .bat files CRLF on every checkout, and keep their text plain ASCII.
*.bat text eol=crlf
